--- !u!114 &1098233640
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1098233639}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a153f0678737c604b9f76a7fe6f63737, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Qandeel.Runtime::Qandeel.ControlRoom
  view: {fileID: 10889680}
  tourText:
  - This is the real Barakah coast, from a Copernicus Sentinel-2 satellite image. ENEC's offshore monitoring flags a Blue Blubber swarm, and Qandeel starts here, after the warning. In this demo the swarm starts a few hundred metres from the intake mouth and time runs fast; each bell is one modelled jellyfish, drawn larger from the air so it can be seen.
  - The drift planner releases virtual jellyfish into real 2025 Copernicus currents and ERA5 winds. For an alert 15 kilometres north-north-west it gave a 44 percent chance of arrival within 16 to 73 hours and switched the curtain on 13 hours after the alert.
  - Blowers on the western breakwater send air to ten pipes on the seabed across the 967 metre mouth. At the normal setting, about 174 cubic metres of air a minute at about 1.1 bar, the rising bubbles drive a surface current of about 0.45 metres per second out to sea.
  - This is one section, cut away. Air leaves 2 millimetre holes in a 200 millimetre plastic pipe 0.75 metres above the seabed. The plume turns into an outward current near the surface and jellyfish in that layer are held back. Blue Blubber is dense; if a swarm sits deeper than this layer, more get through, shown in red. The sea pilot measures swarm depth first.
  - The intake flow keeps the held jellyfish in a dense patch in front of the middle of the curtain. A workboat sweeps the patch with a floating boom and closes a fabric bag around it. Moored pockets at the breakwater tips stay as a backup.
  - A workboat of 8 to 10 metres with 1 to 2 tonnes of bollard pull takes a closed fabric bag of about 10 tonnes and tows it at 0.2 metres per second, about seven hours round the outer breakwater, to a release point about 3 kilometres north-north-west, where the jellyfish are released alive. Above 1 metre waves the boats stand by; above 2 metres they shelter and the curtain keeps working.
  - Qandeel acts after ENEC's early warning. A drift planner predicts if and when a swarm reaches the Barakah intake. A bubble curtain across the 967 metre mouth holds it at sea, a boat closes a floating boom around the held swarm, and tows it away in a closed bag to release it alive. The curtain is one layer; the existing screens stay as the last line.
  tourVoice:
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: bec37c957497e684489df25e6e6f6a09, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: c205044a4207d0e428f23c804d9d4650, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: f785be8820bae2547a7b9531dbd21ee5, type: 3}
